sched_clock: count from boot, not from INITIAL_JIFFIES

The default sched_clock() multiplies the raw jiffies value, which starts
at INITIAL_JIFFIES (five minutes before the 32-bit wrap).  printk
timestamps therefore start at about 178956670 s on HZ=24 and jump back
to 0 when jiffies wraps.  Subtract INITIAL_JIFFIES, as later kernels do.

diff -Nur linux-2.6.26.orig/kernel/sched_clock.c linux-2.6.26/kernel/sched_clock.c
--- linux-2.6.26.orig/kernel/sched_clock.c	2026-11-21 11:50:41.015303375 +0100
+++ linux-2.6.26/kernel/sched_clock.c	2026-11-21 11:50:41.017661796 +0100
@@ -242,5 +242,6 @@
  */
 unsigned long long __attribute__((weak)) sched_clock(void)
 {
-	return (unsigned long long)jiffies * (NSEC_PER_SEC / HZ);
+	return (unsigned long long)(jiffies - INITIAL_JIFFIES)
+					* (NSEC_PER_SEC / HZ);
 }
